  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "examples/harness_auto_preview.py",
      "line": 6,
      "severity": "P3",
      "title": "mean_chunk_score raises ZeroDivisionError on empty input despite docstring promising 0.0",
      "explanation": "The new fixture declares in its docstring (line 5) that \"an empty list should produce 0.0\", but the implementation on line 6 is `return sum(scores) / len(scores)`. For an empty list, `sum([])` is 0 and `len([])` is 0, so the division raises `ZeroDivisionError` instead of returning 0.0. The declared empty-input contract and the implementation disagree. This matches the accepted maintainer guidance for examples/**/*.py: verify that a function's declared empty-input behavior matches its implementation. The function is a standalone fixture with no repository callers (search_code for `mean_chunk_score` returns only its own definition), so the impact is confined to the fixture's own documented contract rather than production call paths; severity is therefore low. The file compiles cleanly (check-001), so this is a runtime contract defect, not a syntax issue.",
      "trigger": "Call `mean_chunk_score([])` (the empty-list case the docstring explicitly describes). Expected per docstring: returns 0.0. Actual: raises `ZeroDivisionError: division by zero` at line 6.",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "check-001"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "id": "finding-2f4f086bcaf8f32e"
    }
  ],
  "verdicts": [
    {
      "finding_index": 1,
      "verdict": "supported",
      "reason": "Head lines 5-6 confirm the docstring promises 0.0 for an empty list while the body is `sum(scores) / len(scores)`, which raises ZeroDivisionError for []. File is new at head (absent at base), so the defect is introduced by this diff. Trigger is deterministic and matches the code; severity P3 is reasonable given no production callers."
    }
